\author{Compiled by Claude Sonnet 5.5 (claude-sonnet-5-5) for Andr\'as Kornai}
\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Fania (ISO 639-3 \texttt{fni}; Glottocode fani1244), also called Kulaale (speakers call themselves Kulaawe), is a Bua-group Adamawa language of the Guéra region of Chad, spoken in about a dozen villages. Estimates of speakers run from about 1,000 to 1,100 (Global Recordings Network gives a population of 2,900). It is endangered: in towns such as Sarh and N'Djamena it is usually not passed on to children. Northern and southern dialects differ considerably.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
None found. Joshua Project lists no written form.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Lionnet, Language Snapshot on Fania/Kulaale, Language Documentation and Description: \url{https://www.lddjournal.org/article/id/1213/}
\item Glottolog entry for Fania: \url{https://glottolog.org/resource/languoid/id/fani1244.bigmap.html}
\item Numerals page for Fania (MPI Leipzig channumerals): \url{https://lingweb.eva.mpg.de/channumerals/Fania.htm}
\item Wikipedia article: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fania_language}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
Joshua Project reports no Bible translation: \url{https://www.joshuaproject.net/index.php/languages/fni}

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
Global Recordings Network language page: \url{https://globalrecordings.net/en/language/fni} (the content of recordings was not checked).

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Fania has no known written standard or school use, and few documented materials. A curriculum would first need an agreed orthography and community-led materials; the Lionnet snapshot is the best starting point for the linguistic facts.

\end{document}
