\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Foi (ISO 639-3 \texttt{foi}; Glottocode foii1241), also written Foe or Mubi River, is one of the two East Kutubuan languages of the Trans-New Guinea family, spoken along Lake Kutubu and the Mubi River in Papua New Guinea. Wikipedia gives 6,000 speakers (2015); Joshua Project gives about 9,800 Foi people.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
None found.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Murray Rule, \emph{The Culture and Language of the Foe} (1993), catalogued in Glottolog with dictionary and grammar-sketch document types (224 pages); lexical content is not confirmed: \url{https://glottolog.org/resource/reference/id/50152}
\item Wikipedia article on Foi: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foi_language}
\item Wikipedia article on Kutubuan languages: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kutubuan_languages}
\item Everyculture.com, Foi orientation (ethnographic background): \url{https://www.everyculture.com/Oceania/Foi-Orientation.html}
\end{itemize}
No standalone Foi dictionary was found.

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Foi New Testament (Rule, 1978; reprint Bible Society of Papua New Guinea, 1989), catalogue record at ANU: \url{https://openresearch-repository.anu.edu.au/items/32d5c89d-1684-4ff4-a29a-5a421e9ab4d9/full}
\item Joshua Project page for the Foi people: \url{https://www.joshuaproject.net/index.php/people_groups/11220}
\end{itemize}

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
None found.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Foi is not known to be taught in schools. The existing printed materials are a New Testament and an older ethnography/language book, so a curriculum would depend on new community-led materials; the 1978 New Testament shows an established written form.

\end{document}
